1. THE RULE FOR EVERY FUTURE COMMIT

Every ordinary commit added to the main branch after the attested commit named
in section 3 carries a Signed-off-by: trailer naming the human who submits it.
Write it with:

    git commit -s

The sign-off names the human who certifies, not a tool. For the maintainer it is
one of these two addresses:

    0xJ1nn <92670540+0xJ1nn@users.noreply.github.com>
    The ezCORE maintainers <maintainers@ezcore.dev>

The second is a project address for the maintainer and for nobody else. Every
other contributor signs off under the address that identifies them.

Only the person named may add a trailer. Tools and automated assistants cannot
certify, because they hold no rights to license, so they never sign off. Where a
commit was produced with automated assistance, the human who submits it is the
certifier, has reviewed the work, and discloses the assistance as described in
CONTRIBUTING.md.

Merge commits carry no trailer. A merge adds no content of its own, since every
commit it brings in is already in the history.

2. ASSISTED-BY IS A DISCLOSURE, NOT A RIGHTS CLAIM

An Assisted-by: trailer, and any Co-Authored-By: trailer naming a tool or an
automated assistant, records how a commit was produced. Neither is a signature
or an attestation, and neither claims authorship or copyright on anyone's
behalf. Only Signed-off-by: carries the certificate above. Disclosing
assistance neither weakens the certifier's statements nor adds a rights holder.

