4. WHY THE HISTORY WAS NOT REWRITTEN

A commit that lacks a trailer could be given one by rewriting it. I considered
that and rejected it. Rewriting changes the hash of that commit and of every
commit after it: it breaks the release tags v0.1.0, v0.1.1 and v0.2.0,
invalidates every open pull request and every outside reference to a commit, and
restates work that was really done. It would also place a certification into
commit messages in a form I never wrote at the time, which defeats the purpose
of a certificate.

A dated statement covers the same ground, says plainly when it was made, and
leaves the public history exactly as it happened. It is the ordinary way a
project formalises the DCO after the fact. The evidence is in
docs/DCO-ATTESTATION.md, with the command anyone can run to confirm that the
rule has held since this date.
